                        "tags": [
                            "accusative",
                            "singular"
                        ],
                        "form": "den Monophysitismus"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5bc7349f-5b23-31c8-970a-d53f30856def",
                        "entry_id": "ca4b7040-4725-33be-a9ff-e1ff6b306001",
                        "glosses": [
                            "der Glaube, Jesus Christus sei vollkommen göttlich und habe nur eine göttliche – keine menschliche – Natur gehabt (im Gegensatz zur Zwei-Naturen-Lehre)"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "5ba124e6-72cf-4287-987e-dcbe68b306fe",
                                "sense_id": "5bc7349f-5b23-31c8-970a-d53f30856def",
                                "text": "„Obwohl das 6. in der Reihe der ökumenischen Konzile, das 3. Konzil in Konstantinopel, 680–681 den Monophysitismus endgültig verurteilte, gelang es ihm, in einigen Kirchen bis heute zu überleben: sowohl die armenische Kirche als auch die Syrisch-Orthodoxe Kirche (Jakobiten), die Äthiopisch-orthodoxe und die koptische Kirche vertreten die Position des Monophysitismus zumindest in der etwas abgeschwächten Form des \"Miaphysitismus\", weil sie zwar die eine, aber immerhin die eine doppelte – göttliche und menschliche – Natur Christi bekennen.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a2008f75-dfeb-43f0-af86-2c00c2eaf834",
                        "source_entry_id": "ca4b7040-4725-33be-a9ff-e1ff6b306001",
                        "source_sense_id": "5bc7349f-5b23-31c8-970a-d53f30856def",
                        "target_lang_word": "monophysitism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "monophysitism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                        "entry_id": "4cdd1e45-0d03-3c9f-b2e2-de8cb2645678",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A Christological doctrine, generally considered heterodox or heretical, holding that Jesus Christ has a single nature, which is either wholly divine or partially human and partially divine."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bfb3e3ea-a47c-43dd-89cc-13532db46752",
                                                "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                                "text": "To claim that Jesus did not have human feelings amounts to monophysitism."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "932d4b6c-09c1-4d19-afd9-febcd108679b",
                                                "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                                "text": "But of monophysitism, Eutychianism, mixture or fusion of the natures, transformation of the one nature into the other, or the absorption of humanity into divinity Schwenckfeld was accused, as previously observed."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "691036e2-4624-4468-bfbe-21c4210d5e65",
                                                "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                                "text": "A third inadequate view is called monophysitism […] The primary advocate of this view in the early church was Eutyches […] [who] taught that Jesus was a mixture of divine and human elements in which both were somewhat modified to form one new nature."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fee86de2-e371-4833-bc04-732e998ad774",
                                                "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                                "text": "The other major dissenting group, the Monophysites […] rejected the Orthodox doctrine that Christ’s divine and human natures were separate. In practice, they usually even went further and stated that Christ had a single, divine nature. Monophysitism became the doctrine of the Coptic Church in Egypt, which made up the vast majority of Egyptians."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cc11a111-7eb8-4b67-b7c7-39dfaddb2c7e",
                                                "sense_id": "e0e19043-b006-383a-891e-0582ce210f0d",
                                                "text": "Severus of Antioch became the new leader of Monophysitism after Chalcedon […] The Monophysites saw the Logos as he had become incarnate, and possessed his humanity and divinity in one new nature that combined both."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b5aaa39d-0078-3e10-aeb9-f7be95973d62",
                                        "entry_id": "4cdd1e45-0d03-3c9f-b2e2-de8cb2645678",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The beliefs and practices of the Oriental Orthodox Church."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "derogatory",
                                            "sometimes",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b6a466ae-15b4-4420-992a-141ef6bf50cf",
                                                "sense_id": "b5aaa39d-0078-3e10-aeb9-f7be95973d62",
                                                "text": "Aramaic was the vernacular language of Syria until it was replaced by Arabic. Syriac remained the ecclesiastical language of Monophysitism."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "01acebc6-3aa2-4987-b351-66d7f4f42967",
                        "source_entry_id": "ca4b7040-4725-33be-a9ff-e1ff6b306001",
